  {
    "pkheadingid": 780,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 717,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118802,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 52,
        "headingId": 717,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:42:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 774,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 711,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118803,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 52,
        "headingId": 711,
        "headingText": "<p>The bone marrow is usually markedly hypercellular, although exceptions may occur. The blasts are medium-sized to large, with high N:C&nbsp;ratios, pale grey-blue cytoplasm, round or slightly indented nuclei, and one to two distinct nucleoli. Some cases are characterized by blasts with azurophilic granules and/or Auer rods. In other cases, blasts resemble lymphoblasts and lack azurophilic granules. A defining feature of this AML type is the presence of granulocytic maturation, albeit such cells should by convention constitute &lt;&nbsp;10% of cellular elements in the bone marrow.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5>Immunophenotype</h5>\r\n\r\n<p>The blasts express cytoplasmic myeloperoxidase (&ge;&nbsp;3%) and two or more myeloid-associated antigens such as CD13, CD33, and CD117. Blasts are often positive for CD34 and HLA-DR. Markers of granulocytic maturation (e.g. CD15, CD65) and monocytic differentiation (e.g. CD11c, CD14, CD36, and CD64) are usually negative {10637492; 10910899; 12646939; 15875082; 18803279}. CD7 and/or CD56 expression is detected in as many as one&nbsp;third of cases. Expression of CD2, CD5, CD19, c/sCD22, and cCD79a is rare {10637492; 10910899; 12646939; 15875082; 18803279}. CD3 is typically negative.</p>\r\n\r\n<h5>Cytochemistry</h5>\r\n\r\n<p>Blasts are positive for myeloperoxidase and/or Sudan Black&nbsp;B (&ge;&nbsp;3%). They are usually negative for naphthol AS-D CAE.</p>\r\n&nbsp;",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:42:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 775,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 712,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118804,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 52,
        "headingId": 712,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>See above.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:42:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 776,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 713,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118805,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 52,
        "headingId": 713,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p>Approximately two&nbsp;thirds of cases have a normal karyotype. The rest have either single trisomies (e.g. trisomy&nbsp;8, 11, 13, or&nbsp;21) or rare structural abnormalities {15527902}. Frequently mutated genes include <em>DNMT3A</em> (~30%), <em>RUNX1</em> (~30%), <em>ASXL1</em> (~25%), <em>IDH2</em> (~25%), and <em>IDH1</em> (~20%); less commonly mutated genes include <em>TET2</em>, <em>STAG2</em>, <em>NRAS</em>, <em>SRSF2</em>, <em>U2AF1</em>, <em>SF3B1</em>, and <em>ZRSR2</em>. <em>FLT3</em> internal tandem duplication (ITD) and tyrosine kinase domain (TKD) mutations are present in 15% and 10% of cases, respectively {27285584; 29961066}. It should be noted that some of the aforementioned mutations now qualify for myelodysplasia-related AML (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/63/47\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>Acute myeloid leukaemia, myelodysplasia-related</em>).</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:42:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 779,
    "bookid": 63,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 716,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 118806,
        "bookId": 63,
        "chapterId": 52,
        "headingId": 716,
        "headingText": "\r\n\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> &ge;&nbsp;20% blasts in bone marrow and/or blood, with cytochemical evidence of myeloid differentiation (&ge;&nbsp;3%) and limited (&lt;&nbsp;10%) morphological features of granulocytic maturation; positive for at least two myeloid-associated markers (e.g. CD13, CD33, CD117); not fulfilling criteria for AML types with defined genetic alterations; not fulfilling diagnostic criteria for myeloid neoplasm post cytotoxic therapy.</p>\r\n\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-03-01T12:42:19"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 777,
